        fp: "A commercial warehouse owner entered into a written contract with an industrial paint contractor to scrape and paint the corrugated steel exterior of a large warehouse for $30,000, payable upon completion. The contract provided that work would begin on August 1 and be finished by August 31. On August 15, after the contractor had completely scraped the old paint and applied primer to the entire structure, an accidental fire originated in an adjoining, unrelated manufacturing plant. The fire quickly spread across the property line, destroying the entire warehouse structure through no fault of either party. The contractor had incurred $12,000 in labor and material costs, which had increased the value of the standing warehouse prior to the fire. The owner refused to make any payment to the contractor, arguing that full performance had become impossible and that the owner had derived no lasting physical benefit from the work.",
