        exp: "Rule: A critical distinction exists under the doctrine of impossibility between a contract to construct a brand-new building and a contract to repair or remodel an existing building. In a repair or remodeling contract, the continued existence of the building is a basic underlying assumption of the agreement. If the existing structure is destroyed by fire without the fault of either party, both parties are discharged from further executory contractual duties under the doctrine of impossibility. However, under restitution principles (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 377), the contractor is entitled to recover in quasi-contract (quantum meruit) for the reasonable value of the labor and materials furnished to the structure prior to its destruction, as the work had been incorporated into the owner's property before the casualty (Option C). Option A is incorrect because the rule denying restitution applies to contracts for *new* construction where the builder bears the risk of loss until delivery, not repair contracts on existing structures. Option B is incorrect because contract damages are unavailable where the owner committed no breach and performance was discharged by impossibility. Option D is incorrect because expectation damages are barred when performance is excused by casualty."
